WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.840
Welcome to the FAMU College of Education Action Research Navigator.

00:03.840 --> 00:08.160
This guided workspace helps you move from a classroom concern to a complete, practical

00:08.160 --> 00:09.520
action research plan.

00:09.520 --> 00:11.520
It does not invent your study for you.

00:11.520 --> 00:16.240
Instead, it organizes your thinking, offers focused prompts, and turns your own decisions

00:16.240 --> 00:20.340
into an implementation blueprint you can use with your instructor or research team.

00:20.340 --> 00:24.080
Begin by creating a project and describing the problem of practice.

00:24.080 --> 00:28.860
Keep the concern specific, observable, and connected to your own educational setting.

00:28.860 --> 00:32.860
The prompts on each page help you distinguish what you have actually noticed from assumptions

00:32.860 --> 00:34.080
you may be making.

00:34.080 --> 00:37.960
Your work saves automatically in this browser, so you can move through the Navigator over

00:37.960 --> 00:39.320
several sessions.

00:39.320 --> 00:42.960
Next, select the research approach that fits your purpose.

00:42.960 --> 00:45.600
Descriptive research helps you understand what is happening.

00:45.600 --> 00:50.640
Evaluative research examines how well an existing program or practice is working.

00:50.640 --> 00:53.760
Analytical research explores relationships and explanations.

00:53.760 --> 00:57.280
The Navigator then helps you name a theoretical or conceptual lens.

00:57.280 --> 00:59.780
That lens should clarify what you pay attention to.

00:59.780 --> 01:01.940
It should not simply decorate the study.

01:01.940 --> 01:03.880
Now develop the action research question.

01:03.880 --> 01:08.220
A strong question identifies the learners or setting, the instructional action, and the

01:08.220 --> 01:09.860
change you will examine.

01:09.860 --> 01:15.100
From there, build an action plan with manageable steps, responsibilities, and success indicators.

01:15.100 --> 01:16.940
The goal is not to redesign everything at once.

01:16.940 --> 01:21.660
It is to make one purposeful change, observe it carefully, and learn from what happens.

01:21.660 --> 01:24.380
The data plan asks you to gather more than one view of the problem.

01:24.380 --> 01:28.500
You might combine student work, a short perception measure, and structured observation notes.

01:28.500 --> 01:32.940
This process, called triangulation, strengthens your interpretation, complete the ethics checklist

01:32.940 --> 01:38.220
before collecting anything, protect privacy, minimize risk, use informed consent when required,

01:38.220 --> 01:40.620
and follow all institutional and district guidance.

01:40.620 --> 01:42.300
The Navigator is a planning tool.

01:42.300 --> 01:45.580
It does not replace faculty review or an IRB decision.

01:45.580 --> 01:49.820
Use the literature and timeline sections to connect your plan with prior scholarship and

01:49.820 --> 01:51.740
make the work realistic.

01:51.740 --> 01:55.220
Record useful sources and explain how each one informs your action.

01:55.220 --> 02:00.740
Then schedule preparation, implementation, data collection, analysis, and reflection.

02:00.740 --> 02:05.320
During the cycle, use the research journal to capture decisions, surprises, evidence,

02:05.320 --> 02:07.660
and adjustments while they are still fresh.

02:07.660 --> 02:12.240
In the analysis section, explain how you will organize and interpret each source of evidence.

02:12.240 --> 02:16.900
Look for patterns across sources, including evidence that challenges your expectations.

02:16.900 --> 02:18.540
Describe limitations, honestly.

02:18.540 --> 02:22.460
Action research supports local improvement, so avoid claiming that one classroom cycle

02:22.460 --> 02:24.260
proves a universal cause.

02:24.260 --> 02:28.540
Then, choose useful deliverables for your audience, such as a faculty briefing, an instructional

02:28.540 --> 02:30.740
resource, or a second-cycle plan.

02:30.740 --> 02:34.420
The review screen checks completeness across the entire project.

02:34.420 --> 02:35.740
Resolve missing sections.

02:35.740 --> 02:40.060
Read the study as one connected argument, and make sure the question, action, evidence,

02:40.060 --> 02:41.660
and analysis all align.

02:41.660 --> 02:46.300
When the project is ready, the Navigator assembles your action research implementation blueprint.

02:46.300 --> 02:49.380
Print it or save it as a PDF for discussion and revision.

02:49.380 --> 02:53.500
The blueprint is the product of your work – a clear, ethical, evidence-informed plan

02:53.500 --> 02:56.660
acting, observing, reflecting, and improving again.
